# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# The app answers at /app, the file behind it is still app.html, and NO RULE
# HERE MAKES THAT HAPPEN. It must stay that way.
#
# There was a rule — `/app  /app.html  200` — and it took the app down with
# ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS on every hostname while the landing page loaded fine.
# Pages serves any .html asset at its extensionless path and 308s the .html form
# onto it, /app.html -> /app, exactly as it does /index.html -> /. It applies
# that canonical redirect to the *target of a rewrite* as well as to the
# incoming request. So /app rewrote to /app.html, the asset layer redirected
# /app.html back to /app, and the browser walked that circle until it gave up.
#
# The rule was written out on the reasoning that a default no file in the
# repository records is a behaviour nobody can check. That reasoning is sound
# and the conclusion was still wrong: the rule was redundant with the platform
# going in and fatal coming out. The check belongs in a check, so it lives in
# tools/check/site-check.mjs, which now fails if any rule maps /app again.
#
# /app.html keeps working for every link that was ever shared — Pages 308s it to
# /app, and with no rule in the way that is where it stops.

# The only path rule, and it exists for a shape of URL that people will keep typing
# for years: the site lived at github.io/RealtimeClipboard/ for its whole life
# before this domain, so the repo name is baked into every link that was ever
# shared, every bookmark, and every search result. GitHub itself 301s those to
# the apex with the prefix stripped — but only for as long as the old Pages site
# exists, and it does not: it 404s, and the tombstone workflow that was to
# replace it was never run and is now deleted. So this rule is
# no longer the backstop it was written as — it is the only thing left, and it
# catches only requests that reach this host. Someone who reconstructs the old
# shape here lands correctly; someone following an old github.io link does not
# arrive at all. Keep it: it costs nothing and it is all there is.
#
# :splat carries the rest of the path through, so /RealtimeClipboard/help/ lands
# on /help/ rather than on the homepage. A redirect that drops the path is worse
# than no redirect: it looks like it worked and silently loses the destination.
/RealtimeClipboard/*  /:splat  301

# icons/ and social/ moved under assets/. These two rules are for consumers that
# hold the OLD url and cannot be edited:
#
#   - og:image is an absolute URL, and Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack and X cache the
#     card they scraped against the link that was shared. Every post that already
#     exists still asks for /social/og-card.png. Without this it 404s and the
#     preview goes blank on links nobody can go back and fix.
#   - an installed PWA holds the manifest it was installed with, so it may still
#     ask for /icons/*. A 301 is cheaper than a re-install.
#
# Both are permanent — the files are never coming back to those paths.
/social/*  /assets/social/:splat  301
/icons/*   /assets/icons/:splat   301

# NOT here: www -> apex. `_redirects` matches paths, not hostnames, so it cannot
# express it. Both hostnames are attached to the same Pages project and would
# otherwise serve the same content at two URLs. The redirect is a zone-level
# Redirect Rule in the Cloudflare dashboard instead.
